u.s forces are exiting afghanistan after  a 20-year mission that became known  as america's longest war today biden  said the military campaign will end  august 31st  while the u.s will not stay involved in  afghanistan militarily  biden says diplomatic and humanitarian  work  will continue in an effort to protect  the thousands of afghan interpreters and  translators who helped u.s and nato  forces  biden announced that the u.s military  will operate flights throughout august  to evacuate them and their families  they will be taken to an unannounced  safe country as they wait for their  visas  to be approved now the taliban are going  from strength to strength in afghanistan  taking over more  territory and that process has only  accelerated as foreign troops leave  biden's message after 20 years u.s and  nato no longer have a job to do  in afghanistan the future of afghanistan  must be determined by  afghans and that includes in 2021  just as it did in 2001 dealing with the  taliban  here is the u.s president our military  mission afghanistan will conclude  on august 31st the drawdown is  proceeding in a secure and orderly way  prioritizing the safety of our troops as  they depart  our military commanders advised me that  once i made the decision to end the war  we needed to move swiftly to conduct the  main elements of the drawdown  and in this context speed is safety  and thanks to the way in which we have  managed our withdrawal  no one no one us forces or any forces  have uh  have been lost conducting our drawdown  differently would have certainly come  with the  increased risk of safety to our  personnel  that was the u.s president there earlier  i spoke with trida parsi  he's executive vice president at the  quincy institute for responsible  statecraft he is an authority on  geopolitics from iran  to saudi arabia to afghanistan and i  asked him if there is  any positive news in this announcement  given that the taliban  is resurging in afghanistan  i think it's important to recognize that  the taliban was resurging  and would be resurging regardless as to  whether the united states would be  leaving or not after 20 years  we have now people saying that the  afghan government cannot stand  alone for six months well after a  20-year investment and they can only  hold out for six months  what does that tell us it tells us that  another year another two years another  20 years  will not make a difference and that is  bringing the united states to a point in  which it has to decide  does it want to continue to engage in an  endless  unwinnable war or is it going to  withdraw  and recognize that the original  objective of defeating the al-qaeda  there  has been achieved and settle for that  yeah the original objective the mission  of going into afghanistan the president  said today was to rid the country of the  terrorists  who had hit the united states but wasn't  that mission accomplished in 2011  when osama bin laden was killed  i think in general uh had it just been  an objective  to go after the taliban and al-qaeda  there  it would have been achieved much earlier  perhaps even earlier than 2011 mindful  of the fact that  bin laden was killed in pakistan not in  afghanistan  i think this is a typical case of  mission creep  in which we originally went in because  of al-qaeda attacking the united states  and then it dwarfed into a  nation-building project  counter-intelligence a counterinsurgency  and it's just been dragging on  this is part of the reason why the  united states should be extremely  selective  when it comes to using force outside of  its own borders  and be extremely selective when it comes  to uh the type of military interventions  that we're seeing here  because once it is started usually there  are very  very few good options left and you will  be forced to only choose between bad  options i heard one u.s lawmaker today  say that the this war would have turned  out much differently  had there not been the invasion of iraq  two years after the start of the war in  afghanistan and this congressman said  that what happened is you had the u.s  trying to focus on two wars neither of  them got complete  attention and you see that mission creep  began as you just said  um is the failure in afghanistan if you  want to call it that  does it have something to do with iraq  um i'm not so convinced by that because  at the end of the day  even if the united states had not gone  into iraq and clearly going into iraq  did not help  but had it not gone into iraq it would  still have been a very difficult mission  because  nation building in afghanistan is not  what the united states is equipped to do  you can take a look at iraq see how  badly nation building went there  as well the problem is that from the  very beginning the mission actually was  limited to defeating the  al-qaeda and then it morphed into  something much much bigger  had that not happened we would be in a  very different situation today you know  the the first questions for the  president from reporters today  were are you handing over afghanistan to  the taliban  were the past two decades in afghanistan  were they worth it  what would be your answers well i think  the premise of that question is quite  incorrect  handing the afghanistan over to the  taliban  presumes that the united states is in  control of afghanistan the united states  is not in control afghanistan and has  not been in control of afghanistan  that's been the problem from the very  beginning  a lot of folk seems to have the  impression that afghanistan some way  somehow is the 51th state of the united  states  and now the by the administration or  whoever had been trump would be handing  it over to someone else  this was never the united states this  was their country  and the us was never in control of it i  think uh uh  assumptions as such has really made the  conversation in the united states  much more difficult about what type of  irrational choices  it can choose between and moving forward  what what will you tell the  administration what should we expect in  the next year in afghanistan um the  government with with the taliban and of  course there's the role of pakistan  and even the role of china to consider  i think it is essential that when the  military mission is now ended  that the united states remains engaged  diplomatically to try to help stabilize  um afghanistan it's going to be very  challenging but one thing the united  states can and should do  is to encourage the regional states the  neighboring states  to really step up and their incentives  to do so has now  increased as a result of the united  states leaving  for instance if you take a look at what  the iranians have been doing in many  ways the iranians had shared interests  with the united states and afghanistan  and opposed the taliban but as long as  the united states was in afghanistan and  was seen as a threat by the iranians the  iranians did not  collaborate with the united states and  actually in many ways undermined the  united states deliberately  fearing that if they didn't the united  states would have greater bandwidth to  attack iran  with the united states not being present  there this dynamic changes and their  opportunities for collaboration and  diplomacy grows but it takes  initiative leadership and participation  by the united states so i think it's  essential  that just because the troops are back  home does not mean that the diplomatic  mission in any way shape or form should  and in fact it should be ramped up  yeah that is exactly what the president  said earlier today  trader parsi we appreciate your time and  your insights tonight thank you
 

